; =============================================================================
; Advanced Loop Optimizations for E-Graph (ONE PASS Global Optimization)
; =============================================================================
;
; This module provides advanced loop transformations:
; - Loop peeling (peel first/last iterations)
; - Loop fusion (combine adjacent loops)
; - Loop fission (split loops)
; - Induction variable optimization
; - Loop-carried dependency analysis
;
; These optimizations are modeled in the e-graph so they can interact with
; other optimizations globally in ONE pass.
;
; =============================================================================
; Loop Peeling
; =============================================================================
; Peeling extracts iterations from loop to enable optimization.
; Peel first iteration: execute iter 0 separately, then loop from 1.

; BoundedTheta where first iteration can be peeled
; PeeledLoop represents a loop with first iteration extracted
; (BoundedTheta n body init) with n > 1 can become:
; let first_result = Subst(body, 0, init) in BoundedTheta(n-1, body, first_result)

; For small loops, peeling + unrolling may fully eliminate the loop
; BoundedTheta(5, body, init) = body(BoundedTheta(4, body, init))
; This allows the 4-iteration unroll to kick in

; Peel-by-one for larger bounded loops (allows unrolling to continue)
(rule ((= e (BoundedTheta 5 body init)))
      ((union e (Subst body 0 (BoundedTheta 4 body init)))))

(rule ((= e (BoundedTheta 6 body init)))
      ((union e (Subst body 0 (BoundedTheta 5 body init)))))

(rule ((= e (BoundedTheta 7 body init)))
      ((union e (Subst body 0 (BoundedTheta 6 body init)))))

(rule ((= e (BoundedTheta 8 body init)))
      ((union e (Subst body 0 (BoundedTheta 7 body init)))))

; =============================================================================
; Induction Variable Optimization
; =============================================================================
; Recognize and optimize induction variables.

; Linear induction variable: i = i + stride
; LoopVar in body (Add (LoopVar) (Const stride)) is a linear IV

; Strength reduction for IV * constant in loop body
; If we have: for i in 0..n: use(i * 4)
; Convert to: for j in 0..n*4 step 4: use(j)
; This is handled by the LoopVar strength reduction rules in loop_unroll.egg

; =============================================================================
; Loop Fusion (Combining Adjacent Loops)
; =============================================================================
; Two loops over same range can be fused if independent.
;
; In RVSDG, adjacent Theta nodes with same bounds can potentially be fused.
; This is complex in general (need to prove independence).
;
; Simple case: Two BoundedThetas in sequence with same trip count
; where second doesn't depend on first's result
;
; For now, we rely on the e-graph finding equivalent expressions across
; what would be separate loops in traditional IR.

; =============================================================================
; Loop Fission (Splitting Loops)
; =============================================================================
; Split loop body into independent parts for better cache behavior.
;
; In e-graph, this is less relevant since we don't have explicit loop bodies.
; The RVSDG Theta node represents the entire loop semantically.
; Fission would create multiple Theta nodes from one.
;
; For now, rely on the e-graph's natural expression splitting.

; =============================================================================
; Dead Loop Elimination
; =============================================================================
; Loop whose result is never used can be eliminated.
; This is handled by liveness propagation in datatypes.egg.

; Loop with no side effects and unused result
; (Handled by Dead store elimination and liveness)

; =============================================================================
; Loop Unswitching
; =============================================================================
; Hoist loop-invariant conditionals outside the loop.
;
; Gamma inside Theta where condition is loop-invariant:
; Theta(cond, Gamma(inv_cond, then_body, else_body), init)
; becomes:
; Gamma(inv_cond, Theta(cond, then_body, init), Theta(cond, else_body, init))

(rule ((= e (Theta cond (Gamma (LoopInvariant inv_cond) then_body else_body) init)))
      ((union e (Gamma (LoopInvariant inv_cond)
                       (Theta cond then_body init)
                       (Theta cond else_body init)))))

; BoundedTheta version
(rule ((= e (BoundedTheta n (Gamma (LoopInvariant inv_cond) then_body else_body) init)))
      ((union e (Gamma (LoopInvariant inv_cond)
                       (BoundedTheta n then_body init)
                       (BoundedTheta n else_body init)))))

; =============================================================================
; Loop Collapse
; =============================================================================
; Nested loops with independent bounds can sometimes be collapsed.
; This is complex - for now, rely on e-graph finding equivalences.

; =============================================================================
; Parallel Loop Recognition
; =============================================================================
; For GPU shaders, recognize parallelizable loop patterns.
; This is more about code generation than optimization.

